In this lesson, we'll look at two quick but important rules about powers: the zero exponent and the zero base. Let's start with the zero exponent. Imagine you take any number, say seven, and divide it by itself. Seven divided by seven equals one, right? Now, using the quotient law, that's also the same number raised to the power of zero. So any non-zero number to the power of zero equals one. Seven to the zero is one, negative three to the zero is one, even one hundred to the zero is one. The key phrase there is non-zero, and that leads to a big warning. Zero to the zero is not defined, so we simply leave it undefined. Now flip it around and think about the zero base. If you multiply zero by itself any positive number of times, you still get zero. So zero to the five is zero, zero to the one hundred is zero. The only tricky case is zero to the zero, which, again, is undefined. So remember, anything except zero to the zero is one, zero to any positive power is zero, and zero to the zero is off limits. Now let's see it in action.